Getting Started on Windows

If you are using MANC-Q on a new Windows computer, follow these steps before running the commands in the rest of this README.

Important: The commands below are intended to be run in Windows Command Prompt or PowerShell, not directly as Python code in a Jupyter Notebook.

1. Install Python

MANC-Q requires Python 3.9 or later.

Download and install Python from the official Python website:

https://www.python.org/downloads/

During installation, make sure to select:

Add Python to PATH

After installation, open Command Prompt or PowerShell and check that Python is available:

python --version


You should see something similar to:

Python 3.12.7


If python is not recognised, Python has either not been installed correctly or has not been added to your PATH.

2. Install Git

MANC-Q is hosted on GitHub and uses Git to download the software.

Download and install Git for Windows:

https://git-scm.com/download/win

After installation, close and reopen Command Prompt or PowerShell.

Check that Git is available:

git --version


You should see something similar to:

git version 2.x.x


If git is not recognised, Git has either not been installed correctly or is not available on your PATH.

3. Download MANC-Q

Choose a folder where you want to keep MANC-Q. For example:

cd "C:\Users\<your-username>\Documents"


Then download the MANC-Q repository:

git clone https://github.com/willwas1/manc-q.git


Enter the MANC-Q directory:

cd manc-q


You can check that you are in the correct directory with:

dir


You should see files including:

requirements.txt

4. Install the required Python packages

Install the dependencies with:

python -m pip install -r requirements.txt


Using python -m pip rather than just pip helps ensure that the packages are installed into the Python installation you are using.

5. Check that MANC-Q works

Before analysing your own NMR data, run the built-in demonstration:

python -m mancq demo


The demo generates a synthetic 600 MHz spectrum containing 18 metabolites, quantifies it, and compares the recovered concentrations with the known concentrations.

The demonstration takes approximately 2 minutes.

If the demo completes successfully, MANC-Q is installed correctly.

Using MANC-Q from Jupyter Notebook

Jupyter Notebook is not required to install or run MANC-Q. The commands in this README are normally run from Command Prompt or PowerShell.

However, MANC-Q can also be run from a Jupyter Notebook. Once MANC-Q has been installed, prefix terminal commands with !.

For example:

!python -m mancq demo


To analyse your own data:

!python -m mancq run "path/to/experiment_folder" "path/to/results" --ref-mm 0.5


It is important that Jupyter is using the same Python environment in which MANC-Q was installed. If you receive an error such as:

No module named mancq


your notebook is probably using a different Python environment.

Troubleshooting

'python' is not recognized...

Python is not installed or is not available on your PATH. Reinstall Python and make sure Add Python to PATH is selected during installation.

'git' is not recognized...

Git is not installed or is not available on your PATH. Install Git for Windows and then restart Command Prompt or PowerShell.

Could not open requirements file

You are probably not inside the MANC-Q directory. Run:

cd manc-q


and then:

python -m pip install -r requirements.txt


No module named mancq

MANC-Q has either not been installed correctly, or you are using a different Python environment from the one where it was installed. Check your Python installation with:

python --version
python -m pip --version


If you are using Jupyter, also check which Python your notebook is using:

import sys
print(sys.executable)


Once the installation and demo have completed successfully, continue with Quick start below to analyse your own NMR spectra.
